Consider the Cauchy problem
\begin{equation} \label{eq: cauchy.argmax}
\begin{cases}
\partial_t\mu(t)+\mathrm{div}\left(\bm{\mathsf{P}}^\perp_x\left(\displaystyle\int y \mu_{\bm{\mathit{B}},x}(t, \, \mathrm{d} y)\right)\mu(t)\right)=0 &\text{ on } \mathbb{R}_{\geqslant0}\times\mathbb{S}^{d-1}\\
\mu(0)=\mu_0 &\text{ on } \mathbb{S}^{d-1},
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $\mu_{\bm{\mathit{B}},x}(\, \mathrm{d} y)$ denotes the conditional probability at the $\mathop{\mathrm{arg\,max}}\limits$ of the map $\mathrm{supp}(\mu)\ni y\to \langle \bm{\mathit{B}} x,y\rangle$, namely
\begin{equation*}
    \mu_{\bm{\mathit{B}},x}(\, \mathrm{d} y):=\mu\left( \, \mathrm{d} y \,\bigg|\, y\in \mathop{\mathrm{arg\,max}}\limits_{x'\in \mathrm{supp}(\mu)} \langle \bm{\mathit{B}} x, x'\rangle\right).
\end{equation*}

Yet, even-though for $\mu\in\mathscr{P}_{\text{ac}}(\mathbb{S}^{d-1})$ and $\bm{\mathit{B}}\in\mathscr{M}_{d\times d}(\mathbb{R})$, one has
\begin{equation*}
    \lim_{\beta\to+\infty}\mathscr{A}_{\beta \bm{\mathit{B}}}[\mu](x)=\mathop{\mathrm{arg\,max}}\limits_{y\in \mathrm{supp}(\mu)}\langle \bm{\mathit{B}} x,y\rangle,
\end{equation*}
the following question is open.

Given $\bm{\mathit{B}}\in\mathscr{M}_{d\times d}(\mathbb{R})$ and $\mu_0\in\mathscr{P}(\mathbb{S}^{d-1})$, does the solution to
\begin{equation*}
\begin{cases}
\partial_t \mu^\beta(t)+\mathrm{div}\left(\bm{\mathsf{P}}^\perp_x\left(\mathscr{A}_{\beta \bm{\mathit{B}}}\left[\mu^\beta(t)\right]\right)\mu^\beta(t)\right)=0 &\text{ on } \mathbb{R}_{\geqslant0}\times\mathbb{S}^{d-1}\\
\mu^\beta(0)=\mu_0 &\text{ on } \mathbb{S}^{d-1},
\end{cases}
\end{equation*}
converge, in an appropriate sense, to an appropriate solution to \eqref{eq: cauchy.argmax}, as $\beta\to+\infty$?

